{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>חשן.</b> A garment-ornament worn opposite the heart." "<b>ואפוד.</b> I have not heard nor have I found in the baraita an explanation of its form, and my heart tells me that it is a belt worn from behind, its width like the width of a man's back, like a kind of apron that they call פורצי״נט בְּלַעַ״ז — the kind that noblewomen wear when they ride on horses; so too its construction is below, as it is said ״וְדָוִד חָגוּר אֵפוֹד בָּד״ — this teaches us that the ephod is a belt. And one cannot say that it is nothing but a belt alone, for it is said ״וַיָּשֶׂם עָלָיו אֶת הָאֵפֹד״ and afterward ״וַיַּחְגֹּר אֹתוֹ בְּחֵשֶׁב הָאֵפֹד״, and Onkelos renders it בְּהֶמְיַן אֵפוֹדָא — this teaches us that the חֵשֶׁב is the belt and the ephod is the name of a garment-ornament alone. And one cannot say that it is called ephod on account of the two shoulder-pieces that are in it, for it is said ״שְׁתֵּי כִתְפוֹת הָאֵפֹד״ — this teaches us that the ephod is a name on its own, and the shoulder-pieces are a name on their own, and the חֵשֶׁב is a name on its own. Therefore I say that on account of the lower apron it is called ephod — because one adorns and ornaments with it — as it is said ״וַיֶּאְפֹּד לוֹ בּוֹ״, and the חֵשֶׁב is the belt above it, and the shoulder-pieces are fixed in it. And further, my heart tells me that there is proof that it is a kind of garment, for Jonathan rendered ״וְדָוִד חָגוּר אֵפוֹד בָּד״ (II Samuel 6) — כַּרְדּוּט דְּבוּץ, and he likewise rendered מְעִילִים כַּרְדּוּטִין, in the episode of Tamar sister of Absalom: ״כִּי כֵן תִּלְבַּשְׁנָה בְּנוֹת הַמֶּלֶךְ הַבְּתוּלוֹת מְעִילִים״ (ibid. 13)." "<b>מעיל.</b> It is like a kind of חָלוּק, and so too the כֻּתֹּנֶת — except that the כֻּתֹּנֶת is worn against the flesh, while מְעִיל is called the upper חָלוּק." "<b>תשבץ.</b> Made with settings for ornament, and the מִשְׁבְּצוֹת are like sockets made in gold ornaments as a seat for setting precious stones and pearls, as is said of the stones of the ephod ״מוּסַבֹּת מִשְׁבְּצוֹת זָהָב״, and in לַעַז they call it קשטו״נש." "<b>מצנפת.</b> Like the cap of a hat, which they call קופ״יא בְּלַעַ״ז — for elsewhere he calls them מִגְבָּעוֹת, and they translate them as כוֹבָעִין." "<b>And the sash.</b> It is girded over the tunic, and the ephod is girded over the robe — as we find in the order of their donning: ״וַיִּתֵּן עָלָיו אֶת הַכֻּתֹּנֶת וַיַּחְגֹּר אֹתוֹ בָּאַבְנֵט וַיַּלְבֵּשׁ אֹתוֹ אֶת הַמְּעִיל וַיִּתֵּן עָלָיו אֶת הָאֵפֹד״ (Leviticus 8)." "<b>Holy garments.</b> From the contribution consecrated to My name he shall make them."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {1 "the la'az פורצי״נט is Old French porcenet/pourcenet, a type of riding apron; the Aramaic כרדוט דבוץ is Jonathan's Targum rendering meaning a closely-woven garment", 3 "the la'az קשטו״נש is Old French c(h)astones or escaston, meaning a setting/socket for gems; the word appears with various spellings in the witnesses", 4 "the la'az קופ״יא is Old French cofia/coiffe, a cap or coif"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:55:21.272266378Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}